(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["text-structure-and-purpose_8"] = [{"id":"be947479","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Jazz tap is a dance form that was first developed in African American communities. Jazz tap was heavily influenced by jazz music, which became widely popular in the United States in the 1920s. Tap dancers were inspired by jazz music’s quick rhythms and by the way jazz musicians would make up melodies as they played. As jazz music continued to develop in the 1930s and 1940s, jazz tap evolved with it. Because of jazz music’s influence, jazz tap quickly developed into a dance form that was very different from earlier kinds of tap dance.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Jazz tap is a dance form that was first developed in African American communities. Jazz tap was heavily influenced by jazz music, which","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"became widely popular in the United States in the 1920s. Tap dancers were inspired by jazz music’s quick rhythms and by the way jazz musicians","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"would make up melodies as they played. As jazz music continued to develop in the 1930s and 1940s, jazz tap evolved with it. Because of jazz","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"music’s influence, jazz tap quickly developed into a dance form that was very different from earlier kinds of tap dance.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["It explains why audiences prefer some kinds of music over others.","It discusses the development of a dance form.","It describes how to play a musical instrument.","It emphasizes the popularity of a famous dancer."]},{"id":"93ce86d0","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Curious about how people visually perceive objects in their dreams, Stephen LaBerge and team recruited lucid dreamers — people aware that they’re dreaming as it’s happening — for a research study. These participants were reliably able to signal when they had entered a dream state; the team then observed participants eye movements as they slept. The smoothness with which participants eyes tracked objects in their dreams closely matched how sighted people who are awake visually track objects around them, suggesting to the team that the brain perceives dream objects as the product of something other than pure imagination.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Curious about how people visually perceive objects in their dreams, Stephen LaBerge and team recruited lucid dreamers—people aware that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"they’re dreaming as it’s happening—for a research study. These participants were reliably able to signal when they had entered a dream state; the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"team then observed participants’ eye movements as they slept. The smoothness with which participants’ eyes tracked objects in their dreams","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"closely matched how sighted people who are awake visually track objects around them, suggesting to the team that the brain perceives dream","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"objects as the product of something other than pure imagination.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","choices":["To offer key evidence that undermines LaBerge and team’s initial hypothesis","To show the unexpected result that led LaBerge and team to change the focus of their study","To illustrate an important real-world implication of LaBerge and team’s main finding","To identify a comparable circumstance that helps justify LaBerge and team’s conclusion"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["The smoothness with which participants’ eyes tracked objects in their dreams","closely matched how sighted people who are awake visually track objects around them,"]},{"id":"d69bc408","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from Aphra Behn’s 1689 novel The Lucky Mistake. Atlante and Rinaldo are neighbors who have been secretly exchanging letters through Charlot, Atlante’s sister. [Atlante] gave this letter to Charlot; who immediately ran into the balcony with it, where she still found Rinaldo in a melancholy posture, leaning his head on his hand: She showed him the letter, but was afraid to toss it to him, for fear it might fall to the ground; so he ran and fetched a long cane, which he cleft at one end, and held it while she put the letter into the cleft, and stayed not to hear what he said to it. But never was man so transported with joy, as he was at the reading of this letter; it gives him new wounds; for to the generous, nothing obliges love so much as love.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Aphra Behn’s 1689 novel The Lucky Mistake. Atlante and Rinaldo are neighbors who have been secretly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"exchanging letters through Charlot, Atlante’s sister.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"[Atlante] gave this letter to Charlot; who immediately ran into the balcony with it, where she still found Rinaldo in a melancholy posture, leaning","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"his head on his hand: She showed him the letter, but was afraid to toss it to him, for fear it might fall to the ground; so he ran and fetched a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"long cane, which he cleft at one end, and held it while she put the letter into the cleft, and stayed not to hear what he said to it. But never was","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"man so transported with joy, as he was at the reading of this letter; it gives him new wounds; for to the generous, nothing obliges love so much","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as love.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the delivery of a letter, and then portrays a character’s happiness at reading that letter.","It establishes that a character is desperate to receive a letter, and then explains why another character has not yet written that letter.","It presents a character’s concerns about delivering a letter, and then details the contents of that letter.","It reveals the inspiration behind a character’s letter, and then emphasizes the excitement that another character feels upon receiving that letter."]},{"id":"b4887dae","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Mathematician Claude Shannon is widely regarded as a foundational figure in information theory. His most important paper, “A Mathematical Theory of Communication,” published in 1948 when he was employed at Bell Labs, utilized a concept called a “binary digit” (shortened to “bit”) to measure the amount of information in any signal and determine the fastest rate at which information could be transmitted while still being reliably decipherable. Robert Gallagher, one of Shannon’s colleagues, said that the bit was “[Shannon’s] discovery, and from it the whole communications revolution has sprung.”","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Mathematician Claude Shannon is widely regarded as a foundational figure in information theory. His most important paper, “A Mathematical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Theory of Communication,” published in 1948 when he was employed at Bell Labs, utilized a concept called a “binary digit” (shortened to “bit”) to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"measure the amount of information in any signal and determine the fastest rate at which information could be transmitted while still being","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"reliably decipherable. Robert Gallagher, one of Shannon’s colleagues, said that the bit was “[Shannon’s] discovery, and from it the whole","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"communications revolution has sprung.”","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It presents a theoretical concept, illustrates how the name of the concept has changed, and shows how the name has entered common usage.","It introduces a respected researcher, describes an aspect of his work, and suggests why the work is historically significant.","It names the company where an important mathematician worked, details the mathematician’s career at the company, and provides an example of the recognition he received there.","It mentions a paper, offers a summary of the paper’s findings, and presents a researcher’s commentary on the paper."]},{"id":"df46a2ee","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from Joseph Conrad’s 1907 novel The Secret Agent: A Simple Tale. Mr. Verloc is navigating the London streets on his way to a meeting. Before reaching Knightsbridge, Mr. Verloc took a turn to the left out of the busy main thoroughfare, uproarious with the traffic of swaying omnibuses and trotting vans, in the almost silent, swift flow of hansoms [horse-drawn carriages]. Under his hat, worn with a slight backward tilt, his hair had been carefully brushed into respectful sleekness; for his business was with an Embassy. And Mr. Verloc, steady like a rock —a soft kind of rock — marched now along a street which could with every propriety be described as private.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from Joseph Conrad’s 1907 novel The Secret Agent: A Simple Tale. Mr. Verloc is navigating the London streets on his way to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"a meeting.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Before reaching Knightsbridge, Mr. Verloc took a turn to the left out of the busy main thoroughfare, uproarious with the traffic of swaying","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"omnibuses and trotting vans, in the almost silent, swift flow of hansoms [horse-drawn carriages]. Under his hat, worn with a slight backward tilt,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"his hair had been carefully brushed into respectful sleekness; for his business was with an Embassy. And Mr. Verloc, steady like a rock—a soft","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"kind of rock—marched now along a street which could with every propriety be described as private.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined phrase in the text as a whole?","choices":["It qualifies an earlier description of Mr. Verloc.","It emphasizes an internal struggle Mr. Verloc experiences.","It contrasts Mr. Verloc with his surroundings.","It reveals a private opinion Mr. Verloc holds."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["a soft","kind of rock"]},{"id":"0b5ecf0e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"During Rome’s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren’t as distinct as they seem, as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"During Rome’s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren’t as distinct as they seem,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"scales.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To give a brief overview of public access to libraries throughout Rome’s republican period","To contend that early imperial leaders in Rome wielded too much influence over libraries","To assert that private and early public libraries in ancient Rome had an essential similarity","To call into question the notion that private Roman libraries disappeared during the first century BCE"]},{"id":"c8603ed7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"San Francisco is known for the colorful murals painted on many of its buildings. The densest collection of murals is found on Balmy Alley in the Mission District neighborhood. In the 1970s, Latina artists painted vivid scenes of community life on walls along this block. As the original murals have faded, later generations of artists have painted new ones over them. As a result, Balmy Alley has become a living showcase of San Francisco’s artistic spirit, with its murals reflecting changes in the cultural life of the city.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"San Francisco is known for the colorful murals painted on many of its buildings. The densest collection of murals is found on Balmy Alley in the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Mission District neighborhood. In the 1970s, Latina artists painted vivid scenes of community life on walls along this block. As the original","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"murals have faded, later generations of artists have painted new ones over them. As a result, Balmy Alley has become a living showcase of San","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Francisco’s artistic spirit, with its murals reflecting changes in the cultural life of the city.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To compare the Balmy Alley murals to other murals in San Francisco","To offer an overview of the history and importance of the Balmy Alley murals","To urge people to protect the murals of San Francisco from decay","To describe the rise of mural painting in San Francisco beginning in the 1970s"]},{"id":"56ec23a0","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Hiroshi Senju is known worldwide for his paintings of waterfalls. These paintings are large and tend not to show the entire waterfall. Instead, Senju focuses on just the point where the falling water reaches the pool below, keeping the top of the waterfall out of view. While Senju’s paintings are rooted in art movements originating in the United States, the artist uses traditional Japanese techniques and materials that make his work instantly recognizable.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Hiroshi Senju is known worldwide for his paintings of waterfalls. These paintings are large and tend not to show the entire waterfall. Instead,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Senju focuses on just the point where the falling water reaches the pool below, keeping the top of the waterfall out of view. While Senju’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"paintings are rooted in art movements originating in the United States, the artist uses traditional Japanese techniques and materials that make","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"his work instantly recognizable.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It introduces an artist and then explains some common characteristics of well-known paintings by that artist.","It explains a specific painting technique and then provides examples of artists who use the technique.","It describes a famous painting and then compares it to a lesser-known painting from the same time period.","It gives an opinion on an artist and then suggests multiple reasons why the artist’s work has been largely overlooked."]},{"id":"2903a041","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Using NASA’s powerful James Webb Space Telescope (JWST), Mercedes L ópez-Morales and colleagues measured the wavelengths of light traveling through the atmosphere of WASP-39b, an exoplanet, or planet outside our solar system. Different molecules absorb different wavelengths of light, and the wavelength measurements showed the presence of carbon dioxide (CO₂) in WASP-39b’s atmosphere. This finding not only offers the first decisive evidence of CO₂ in the atmosphere of an exoplanet but also illustrates the potential for future scientific breakthroughs held by the JWST.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Using NASA’s powerful James Webb Space Telescope (JWST), Mercedes López-Morales and colleagues measured the wavelengths of light","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"traveling through the atmosphere of WASP-39b, an exoplanet, or planet outside our solar system. Different molecules absorb different","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"wavelengths of light, and the wavelength measurements showed the presence of carbon dioxide (CO₂) in WASP-39b’s atmosphere. This finding","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"not only offers the first decisive evidence of CO₂ in the atmosphere of an exoplanet but also illustrates the potential for future scientific","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"breakthroughs held by the JWST.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It discusses a method used by some researchers, then states why an alternative method is superior to it.","It describes how researchers made a scientific discovery, then explains the importance of that discovery.","It outlines the steps taken in a scientific study, then presents a hypothesis based on that study.","It examines how a group of scientists reached a conclusion, then shows how other scientists have challenged that conclusion."]},{"id":"d9915c15","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"In 2020, rap artist and professor A.D. Carson published the first peer-reviewed rap album about his experiences with Black masculinity called “i used to love to dream.” Typically in peer review, experts evaluate scholarly articles prior to publication. For Carson’s album, dubbed a “mixtap/e/ssay,” peer review involved both scholars and rap artists. In combining elements of a mixtape album with scholarly essays that connect Carson’s lyrics to historical and contemporary contexts for listeners both inside and outside academia, Carson’s album helped redefine how scholarship is created and shared.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 2020, rap artist and professor A.D. Carson published the first peer-reviewed rap album about his experiences with Black masculinity called “i","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"used to love to dream.” Typically in peer review, experts evaluate scholarly articles prior to publication. For Carson’s album, dubbed a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“mixtap/e/ssay,” peer review involved both scholars and rap artists. In combining elements of a mixtape album with scholarly essays that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"connect Carson’s lyrics to historical and contemporary contexts for listeners both inside and outside academia, Carson’s album helped redefine","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"how scholarship is created and shared.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To compare the relative public impact of scholarly articles and albums","To capture one scholar’s opinion of a new rap album","To explain why a certain rap album is particularly innovative","To describe how each step of the peer review process unfolds"]},{"id":"acb852e7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is from the 1923 poem “Black Finger” by Angelina Weld Grimké, a Black American writer. A cypress is a type of evergreen tree. I have just seen a most beautiful thing, Slim and still, Against a gold, gold sky, A straight black cypress, Sensitive, Exquisite, A black finger Pointing upwards. Why, beautiful still finger, are you black? And why are you pointing upwards?","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from the 1923 poem “Black Finger” by Angelina Weld Grimké, a Black American writer. A cypress is a type of evergreen tree.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"I have just seen a most beautiful thing,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Slim and still,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Against a gold, gold sky,","indent":1},{"type":"line","text":"A straight black cypress,","indent":1},{"type":"line","text":"Sensitive,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Exquisite,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"A black finger","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Pointing upwards.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Why, beautiful still finger, are you black?","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"And why are you pointing upwards?","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["The speaker assesses a natural phenomenon, then questions the accuracy of her assessment.","The speaker describes a distinctive sight in nature, then ponders what meaning to attribute to that sight.","The speaker presents an outdoor scene, then considers a human behavior occurring within that scene.","The speaker examines her surroundings, then speculates about their influence on her emotional state."]},{"id":"d4732483","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"Studying late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century artifacts from an agricultural and domestic site in Texas, archaeologist Ayana O. Flewellen found that Black women employed as farm workers utilized hook-and-eye closures to fasten their clothes at the waist, giving themselves a silhouette similar to the one that was popular in contemporary fashion and typically achieved through more restrictive garments such as corsets. Flewellen argues that this sartorial practice shows that these women balanced hegemonic ideals of femininity with the requirements of their physically demanding occupation.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Studying late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century artifacts from an agricultural and domestic site in Texas, archaeologist Ayana O. Flewellen","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"found that Black women employed as farm workers utilized hook-and-eye closures to fasten their clothes at the waist, giving themselves a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"silhouette similar to the one that was popular in contemporary fashion and typically achieved through more restrictive garments such as corsets.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Flewellen argues that this sartorial practice shows that these women balanced hegemonic ideals of femininity with the requirements of their","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"physically demanding occupation.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To describe an unexpected discovery that altered a researcher’s view of how rapidly fashions among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas changed during the period","To discuss research that investigated the ways in which Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas used fashion practices to resist traditional gender ideals","To evaluate a scholarly work that offers explanations for the impact of urban fashion ideals on Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas","To summarize the findings of a study that explored factors influencing a fashion practice among Black female farmworkers in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century Texas"]},{"id":"b0f7541b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from Herman Melville’s 1857 novel The Confidence-Man. Humphry Davy was a prominent British chemist and inventor. Years ago, a grave American savant, being in London, observed at an evening party there, a certain coxcombical fellow, as he thought, an absurd ribbon in his lapel, and full of smart [banter], whisking about to the admiration of as many as were disposed to admire. Great was the savant’s disdain; but, chancing ere long to find himself in a corner with the jackanapes, got into conversation with him, when he was somewhat ill-prepared for the good sense of the jackanapes, but was altogether thrown aback, upon subsequently being [informed that he was] no less a personage than Sir Humphry Davy.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Herman Melville’s 1857 novel The Confidence-Man. Humphry Davy was a prominent British chemist and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"inventor.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Years ago, a grave American savant, being in London, observed at an evening party there, a certain coxcombical fellow, as he","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"thought, an absurd ribbon in his lapel, and full of smart [banter], whisking about to the admiration of as many as were disposed to","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"admire. Great was the savant’s disdain; but, chancing ere long to find himself in a corner with the jackanapes, got into","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"conversation with him, when he was somewhat ill-prepared for the good sense of the jackanapes, but was altogether thrown","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"aback, upon subsequently being [informed that he was] no less a personage than Sir Humphry Davy.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["It portrays the thoughts of a character who is embarrassed about his own behavior.","It presents an account of a misunderstanding.","It offers a short history of how a person came to be famous.","It explains why one character dislikes another."]},{"id":"97360a00","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"The following text is adapted from Gwendolyn Bennett’s 1926 poem “Street Lamps in Early Spring.” Night wears a garment All velvet soft, all violet blue... And over her face she draws a veil As shimmering fine as floating dew... And here and there In the black of her hair The subtle hands of Night Move slowly with their gem-starred light.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Gwendolyn Bennett’s 1926 poem “Street Lamps in Early Spring.”","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Night wears a garment","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"All velvet soft, all violet blue...","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"And over her face she draws a veil","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It presents alternating descriptions of night in a rural area and in a city.","It sketches an image of nightfall, then an image of sunrise.","It makes an extended comparison of night to a human being.","It portrays how night changes from one season of the year to the next."]}];
